1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:03,719
Picture the most American meal you can think of.

2
00:00:04,269 --> 00:00:06,428
A burger. Fries. A milkshake.

3
00:00:07,678 --> 00:00:09,922
Except the burger isn't really American.

4
00:00:10,622 --> 00:00:13,397
And, strictly speaking, it isn't even ham.

5
00:00:18,497 --> 00:00:22,339
The story starts in Hamburg, Germany, a big port city on the river Elbe.

6
00:00:22,789 --> 00:00:27,312
By the eighteen hundreds, cooks there were serving chopped, seasoned beef, often with onions.

7
00:00:27,662 --> 00:00:29,409
It became known as Hamburg steak.

8
00:00:30,209 --> 00:00:34,166
Then millions of Europeans left through Hamburg's docks, bound for America.

9
00:00:34,516 --> 00:00:35,909
And the food went with them.

10
00:00:37,309 --> 00:00:41,957
In the United States, Hamburg steak was on restaurant menus by the late eighteen hundreds.

11
00:00:42,407 --> 00:00:44,185
But it was a knife-and-fork meal.

12
00:00:44,785 --> 00:00:49,351
What it needed was bread. Something a worker, a farmer, or a fairgoer could eat standing up.

13
00:00:50,751 --> 00:00:52,767
So, who put it between two slices?

14
00:00:53,467 --> 00:00:55,545
Well. That depends who you ask.

15
00:00:56,545 --> 00:00:58,577
Wisconsin says eighteen eighty-five.

16
00:00:58,757 --> 00:01:01,091
New York says eighteen eighty-five, too.

17
00:01:01,271 --> 00:01:03,314
Oklahoma says eighteen ninety-one.

18
00:01:03,494 --> 00:01:07,650
Connecticut says nineteen hundred, and has a Library of Congress nod to show for it.

19
00:01:07,830 --> 00:01:10,431
Texas says sometime around the eighteen eighties.

20
00:01:11,281 --> 00:01:12,837
Nobody has the receipts.

21
00:01:14,537 --> 00:01:16,929
By nineteen oh six, the burger had a problem.

22
00:01:17,429 --> 00:01:24,871
Upton Sinclair's novel The Jungle exposed filthy meatpacking plants, and ground beef, which can hide almost anything, took the blame.

23
00:01:25,421 --> 00:01:27,813
Burgers became a food to be suspicious of.

24
00:01:28,913 --> 00:01:32,894
Then, in nineteen twenty-one, in Wichita, Kansas, White Castle opened.

25
00:01:33,294 --> 00:01:37,032
Everything was white, and stainless steel, and cooked in plain view.

26
00:01:37,432 --> 00:01:38,943
The message: nothing to hide.

27
00:01:40,643 --> 00:01:41,839
Then came the car.

28
00:01:42,589 --> 00:01:47,154
After the Second World War, America built highways, suburbs, and drive-ins.

29
00:01:47,654 --> 00:01:55,815
In nineteen forty-eight, two brothers in San Bernardino, California, cut their menu down to burgers, fries, and shakes, and ran the kitchen like an assembly line.

30
00:01:56,315 --> 00:02:02,837
A milkshake-machine salesman named Ray Kroc saw it, and in nineteen fifty-five he opened a franchise in Des Plaines, Illinois.

31
00:02:03,437 --> 00:02:05,167
You know how it went from there.

32
00:02:06,567 --> 00:02:09,063
The Big Mac arrived in nineteen sixty-seven.

33
00:02:09,463 --> 00:02:14,714
Today, McDonald's alone runs more than forty thousand restaurants, in over a hundred countries.

34
00:02:15,314 --> 00:02:21,450
The burger is so standard that in nineteen eighty-six, The Economist used the Big Mac to compare currencies around the world.

35
00:02:23,050 --> 00:02:25,957
Americans eat roughly fifty billion burgers a year.

36
00:02:26,307 --> 00:02:29,072
That's about three a week, for every person in the country.

37
00:02:29,972 --> 00:02:31,585
And the burger keeps changing.

38
00:02:31,985 --> 00:02:35,356
In twenty sixteen, plant-based patties went mainstream.

39
00:02:35,706 --> 00:02:38,643
Smash burgers took over menus and food trucks.

40
00:02:39,143 --> 00:02:43,065
But the formula hasn't moved much: ground meat, bread, and something on top.

41
00:02:44,865 --> 00:02:47,036
A dish named after a German city.

42
00:02:47,436 --> 00:02:49,421
Claimed by at least five towns.

43
00:02:49,821 --> 00:02:53,278
Rescued by a coat of white paint, and spread by highways.

44
00:02:54,178 --> 00:02:55,908
The burger isn't one invention.

45
00:02:56,508 --> 00:02:59,039
It's a long argument that everyone keeps eating.

